                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Dual Snake is a really simple puzzle game at first glance, but controlling a two headed snake makes the basic idea much more interesting than I expected. I am controlling both heads at the same time and trying to make my way through an underground world and eventually reach the surface. There is no dialogue or complicated story getting in the way, so most of what I learn comes from actually playing. I like that because the game gets straight to its main idea and lets me work things out for myself.\n\nThe two heads are what make the puzzles work. I have to think about where both sides of the snake are going instead of only worrying about one character. I can climb walls, open doors, push blocks and manipulate gravity, so the levels gradually give me more things to consider. Some puzzles are simple enough that I can work them out quickly, while others make me stop and think about how one head can affect the position of the other. I like that the controls are easy to understand even when the puzzles themselves start getting more complicated.\n\nThere is a good amount of content here too. The game has more than 100 official story levels, and the way the mechanics develop means I am not just playing the exact same puzzle repeatedly. Later on I can also play with the heads separated or control a single head, which changes the way I approach certain challenges. That is probably my favourite part of the progression because the game takes its original gimmick and keeps finding ways to build around it rather than abandoning it once I understand it.\n\nI also like the level editor. Once I have finished with the official levels, I can make my own puzzles and share them, which gives the game more value than it would have as a straightforward campaign. The simple presentation actually helps here because the focus stays on the puzzle design rather than trying to distract me with unnecessary features. The game is also completely free, and after the online features were retired the developer moved the online content into permanent offline features, so the level creation side still has a purpose.\n\nThe main weakness for me is that the two headed movement can sometimes be more frustrating than clever. There are moments where I know what I want to do but getting both heads into the right position takes several attempts. That is part of the challenge, but I think some puzzles rely a little too much on getting the movement exactly right. I also found that the lack of dialogue means the game does not give me much of a story to care about. I do not really mind that because the puzzles are clearly the main attraction, but it does make the overall experience feel quite minimal.\n\nI think the game works best when I play it as a pure puzzle challenge rather than expecting a traditional adventure. The lack of dialogue, simple visuals and straightforward presentation might make it feel basic at first, but the actual puzzle mechanics have enough variety to keep things interesting. I especially like when a level forces me to think about both heads in completely different ways and use the environment to solve the problem. The difficulty can become annoying occasionally, but there is usually a satisfying feeling when I finally understand what the level wants me to do.\n\nOverall, Dual Snake is a clever little puzzle game that gets a surprising amount of mileage out of its two headed snake idea. I like the 100 plus story levels, the different movement mechanics, the later alternative ways of controlling the snake and the level editor. It is not especially deep outside of the puzzles, and some movement can be frustrating, but the core idea is strong enough to carry the experience. For a free puzzle game, I think there is a lot here to work through, and it is one of those games where the simple concept ends up being more interesting than it initially looks.\n\nRating: 7.3/10\n",
